The postmortem should teach engineers how to recognize the compromise without shipping the mechanism. I would not include a working redirect, an external destination, or code that collects credentials.

A redacted illustration can show the shape of the defect while leaving the dangerous operation inert:

    if (visitor_matches_unusual_condition(request)) {
        record_local_case_event("unexpected-navigation");
        /* navigation target and action removed from this report */
    }

The condition might be evaluated from a query parameter, referrer class, cookie, device category, path, or geography. That matters during investigation because a site can look normal to the owner while selected visitors experience a different path. Do not infer that a condition proves malicious intent; advertising tags, experiments, localization, and partner integrations can also branch on these signals. Confirm the behavior across a clean browser, a controlled test account, and an isolated network while blocking downloads and refusing to enter real credentials.

Preserve the original response body, headers, script URLs, source maps, DOM snapshot, browser network trace, DNS and content-delivery configuration, deployment artifacts, and access logs. Hash the copies and record collection times. Compare the live page with a signed or otherwise trusted build. Search for newly added inline scripts, unfamiliar third-party tags, changes to tag-manager permissions, modified edge functions, altered service-worker files, and navigation calls assembled from decoded or concatenated strings. Pay attention to code that runs only after a delay or only when a referrer appears to come from a search or advertising source. An unexpected redirect chain, a look-alike sign-in path, or a sudden change in the browser's security warnings should be treated as an incident signal.

Containment begins with a verified rollback or a static maintenance page, followed by disabling the compromised integration and rotating deployment, hosting, DNS, and analytics credentials. Invalidate sessions if a counterfeit page may have received passwords, notify users through a trusted channel, and coordinate with legal and privacy staff. Retain the malicious artifact for analysis rather than deleting it immediately.

Prevention includes protected branches, signed builds, least-privilege service accounts, review for edge and tag-manager changes, a strict content-security policy, dependency pinning, redirect allowlists, and tests that reject absolute or protocol-relative destinations. Monitor navigation and Location headers for unexpected origins. The report should end with owners and dates for each control, not with a reusable hijack recipe.

Treat the captured page as malware evidence, not as code to improve. The training specimen should be inert: it can record that a condition was reached and then throw an error, but it must never navigate or contact a destination.

A useful shape for an inert specimen is:

    if (observed_condition_is_true) {
        record_local_case_event('conditional-navigation-path');
        throw new Error('navigation action removed for training');
    }

The point is to help analysts recognize the surrounding clues. A compromised page may inspect a referrer, path, cookie, device category, language, or geography and then behave differently for a subset of visitors. It may delay the branch until the product grid has rendered, load the logic through a tag manager or service worker, or assemble a suspicious string from fragments. These observations explain a selective incident; they are not a reason to reproduce the behavior. Do not retain the original destination in a classroom copy, and do not test the page with real credentials or an unrestricted browser.

During collection, preserve the response body, headers, DOM, loaded-resource list, source maps, service-worker files, DNS and content-delivery settings, deployment history, access logs, identity-provider alerts, and tag-manager audit trail. Record hashes and timestamps before rolling back. Test from a disposable browser on an isolated network, block downloads, and capture a network trace. Compare behavior across a clean session and a controlled test account. A page that looks normal to an owner but differs for campaign traffic may indicate conditional logic, an advertising experiment, a compromised edge rule, or a third-party integration; the evidence must distinguish them.

Search the known-good build and the captured artifact for unexpected navigation calls, absolute or protocol-relative URLs, newly decoded strings, delayed timers, unfamiliar domains, modified inline scripts, and permissions changes. Check whether a service worker or content-delivery rule can alter navigation before the document loads. Preserve the first malicious copy for analysis instead of deleting it after rollback.

